d green  hatched lines you can see that igm  and igg antibodies for stars cove two  tend to rise and peak at roughly  the same time um and so currently the  testing recommendations don't really  indicate any preference for one antibody  over the other  in terms of of indicating a  recent prior infection  um so  let's go back now to what i was saying  before so we are a clinical laboratory  in brooklyn  um and in march our outbreak started  here in new york  and providers were across the area were  just  scrambling to bring on  some kind of diagnostic testing for  patients  who they thought were either exposed to  or had  sars cove too so our laboratory brought  on pcr testing for sars cove 2 in march  and this next slide shows you our pcr  testing results  so you may be a little bit surprised to  see you know i just will point out that  each month we test  roughly 25 000 um sars co2  pcr specimens so you may be surprised to  see here we started testing in march  and in march the month of march when our  outbreak  was happening here in new york we had a  nearly 60 percent  pcr positivity rate which is truly  almost unfathomable it was a very hard  time to run a clinical laboratory  in in the city of new york um  and you'll see we also that continued  the high positivity rate into april  um where it was over 30 and that's  because  the first half of the month was  basically also very high 50 to 60  percent  and then in the second half of april um  once new york  had sort of uh instituted  more strict shutdown and um quarantined  procedures and  matt encouraged mask wearing and other  social distancing  uh procedures we were effectively able  to flatten the curve in new york which  you can see  we did a very good job of for the summer  months with  very low positivity rates through the  summer  those rates now are maybe a little bit  higher with back to school  but still we're not seeing anything  close  to what we saw at the beginning of this  pandemic  in march and april  so i've told you that we began  pcr testing in march and providers  saw the results of their patients pcr  tests  however in march access to pcr  testing reagents consumables  instrumentation  was very limited not just at our  laboratory but  laboratories across the country and so  providers were not able to test everyone  who  they wanted to test  so naturally seeing  the high 60 positivity rate for pcr  providers wondered well how can i  determine which of my patients  may have previously had  sars sars co2 infection and either been  unable to access  a pcr test or either had it before pcr  testing was even  available widespread and so naturally  providers are used to thinking about  antibodies and so they  immediately started reaching out to  laboratories across the region looking  for  sars code 2 antibody assays and in march  and the beginning of april  there were no fda approved or no fda  eua emergency use authorization  approved antibody tests for  covin however what there were and what  the fda did allow  was manufacturers to distribute antibody  tests  to providers and laboratories  without going through the eua process  because they had a backlog and they  didn't want to be accused i guess  of of hindering testing testing and  access to testing  so effectively what this wound up doing  was  flooding the market with quite frankly  tests that were not  well tested verified tests that  we could not confirm the accuracy of but  providers were desperate and so they  began using many of these tests  lateral flow immunoassays that just  uh you know came to them from mysterious  manufacturers  um and they use these assays at the  point of care  um to make clinical decisions and  i think that very very good many of  these assays were  clinically useless to be quite honest  fortunately in april a number of  manufacturers  got fda eua for  antibody tests more reliable  reviewed by the fda and had received  emergency use authorization  and so our laboratory decided to go with  one of these  fda eua authorized tests that was one of  the first  to market and that was the diasporin  sars code ii igg assay which  detects antibodies against the cor  covid2 uh sars cove to  spike protein which is probably one of  the more immunogenic proteins  by the from the virus so we started  testing in april  of uh this year and every month we test  about 30 000 patients  for antibodies and you'll see in april  and may  our data showed a 45 antibody positivity  rate  from everyone that we tested in the  laboratory  and many of these people were people who  were never symptomatic  but because the prevalence in new york  city  and in particularly in brooklyn and  queens was so high  a very large proportion of our patient  population  tested positive for covet igg antibodies  and this has been fairly consistent we  haven't had  a huge number as you saw in the previous  slide of new infections over the summer  so most of these positive antibody tests  that were seen in september and october  are most likely patients who were  infected during our outbreak  in march and april who still have  itg antibodies  so one thing we did look at uh early in  the course of this pandemic in our  laboratory  um in the month of april was how  um antibody responses were  sort of broken up by age group and so  that's here on the  left panel if you take a look and you  can see that for us  in the month of april the antibody  positive prevalence rate  was relatively consistent for most age  groups except for  a little bump on the left where we see a  slightly higher  slightly higher but significantly higher  uh positivity rate in  patients let's say between the ages of  10 and 28 years of age and this was  interesting to us  because these were patients who we know  do not experience  um usually do not experience ill effects  from a size cove to infection in terms  of symptoms or  or outcomes but it does seem based on  this data  that they do have robust antibody  responses  and make igg antibodies regardless of  that  or maybe because of that again we don't  we don't know for sure at this time  one other thing we looked at here on the  right is we worked with a provider at an  urgent care facility  who worked with us to grade patients  as they came into his facility based on  their symptoms  with zero on the x-axis representing  asymptomatic patients and a four on the  x-axis  representing the most critically ill  patients patients who required either  hospitalization or oxygen support  and and here in this figure we look at  the antibody response in terms of a  semi-quantitative antibody response  uh using the deosorin liaison igg assay  the response of the igg to that symptom  severity and we see in both males and  females  a relation between increased symptoms  severity  and increased levels of of igg  antibodies which maybe  says that more critically ill patients  make have a stronger antibody response  again there's still more to be studied  here  but when we publish this in the journal  diet of diagnostic microbiology and  infectious diseases in  in may it was sort of new information  for everyone  so i want to transition a little bit  from the laboratory to the point of care  because i think that during a pandemic  such as this  point of care testing is very important  uh people are walking into  urgent cares and they want results  available to them before they  leave for the day basically within 20-30  minutes  so there are a number of point-of-care  tests that have received  fda emergency use authorization  including uh one eua point of care  antibody test  and the other 14 split evenly between  point of care molecular and antigen  tests  the point of care molecular tests tend  to be  um hard for facilities to come by  reagents consumables and test  instrumentation is short so the vast  majority of point of care testing  that's performed today in the clinic is  antigen testing and  the three of the the most common point  of care antigen tests  are the kaidel sophia which was the  first to receive fda eua  the bd variety and the abbott binax now  i mentioned a bit earlier just to point  out  that eua and eun are not necessarily  created equally again the fda allowed  for manufacturers to release tests onto  the market  without going through the emergency use  authorization approval process  which is not to say that all eun tests  are  inherently worse than an eua test just  that there's less  oversight of them  so as these sort of point-of-care  antigen testing  kits came onto the market we began to  hear reports that  they weren't very good their sensitivity  was bad  you know there are a lot of false  negatives false positives no one really  had a very good sense of  what was going on and so these are just  two popular press headlines some  recently  you know coronavirus antigen tests quick  and cheap but too often wrong  a rapid virus test falters and people  without symptoms  study fines and so uh you we're seeing a  lot in the popular press  especially about the performance of  antigen tests um  and it's not always necessarily the most  accurate source of information or at  least not the most accurate  interpretation of  what i believe the data actually show  that being said we also see sort of the  other side of this  so just just the other day we i heard  that slovakia has  intends to test its entire population  using uh  antigen testing which i think is is  pretty neat so we'll see i'm sure  um i'm sure we'll see in the coming in  the coming weeks  how that shakes out  um but in order to talk about you know  the accuracy and sensitivity of uh pcr  testing  i uh of antigen testing i think first  we need to talk about  the pcr testing which is sort of the  gold standard now  and i think we need to talk about pcr  testing in terms of sensitivity versus  clinical utility  so i've just summarized a review of 29  studies that look  at pcr and compare it to viral culture  which if you  again if you remember from an earlier  slide  viral culture is generally a measure of  infectious viral particles with the  ideal  the idea being that culture negative  samples  are are non-infectious  so if there's no if you're not able to  grow a virus  in culture from a patient a patient  sample  then you are not like that patient is  not likely to be able to pass  infectious urine on to someone else  so looking at a number of studies and  it always is difficult to compare one  manufacturer's pcr  assay to another but  generally the consensus is that for pcrs  with ct values greater than  30 so weak positive pcrs  there is limited to no culture growth  seen from these specimens so  high positive pcrs generally do not  correlate with the ability to recover  virus from a specimen  which we can then say implies that high  positive pcr-positive specimen specimens  don't correlate to infectivity  necessarily and so why is this the case  um i mentioned also earlier we seem to  now  know that rna shedding for sars co2  lasts a bit longer than we would have  originally expected  so we generally see rna shedding 14 days  and beyond past the onset of symptoms  whereas we don't really see positive  viral culture results beyond about 8  days or so  from  from symptoms  um and so this sort of brings us to uh  you know the joys of running the  clinical laboratory during the global  pandemic  uh in march we were when we first  brought pcr testing online  i got calls every day from clients  wanting to make sure that the pcr test  we were running  was a good pcr test that was sensitive  enough to catch  everything and now sort of as  providers are wrapping their heads  around the implications of  high ct value samples and how they  relate to  rna shedding versus positive culture  results they're now starting to call and  ask  well is maybe your lab's pcr test overly  sensitive  you know i've even had patients call the  lab and say oh i read in the new york  times about ct values  can you tell me about the ct values in  your laboratory uh so you know  there's a lot going on here and a lot  left for us to learn  and unpack about uh pcr testing so i'm  just gonna talk a little bit briefly  about it  but again it won't be a comprehensive  discussion for sure so this is a figure  from  the new england journal of medicine by a  group out of boston  and they sort of compare two  hypothetical tests  so you see that they list the bottom  line is a high analytic sensitivity test  which they're calling a pcr  and the top line is a low analytic  sensitivity test which i  i think you can think of as sort of an  antigen test and the argument here that  the authors are making  is that these high analytic tests these  pcrs we know that resources are limited  tests are limited we can't test serially  a patient  every day so you may because you only  get decreased frequency of testing  you may miss the infectious period of  that patient so for example if you look  at the high analytic  sensitivity line you have an initial  test which is negative before that  patient  has made any virus which is fine that  patient is negative that patient is not  making any virus  but then because of lack of testing or  whatnot by the time a patient gets a  result  after they have symptoms they're in a  post-infectious period  so they're in that period after culture  is no longer positive  however because the pcr test has a high  sensitivity  they've got a pipe they've got a  positive pcr result so the question  remains and i'm not going to answer this  question  certainly but as a provider what do you  do with  a positive pcr result like this from  what we're calling a post-infectious  patient now contrast this to the line  that's labeled low analytic sensitivity  where the argument is if this is a  readily available test that you can just  pump out  thousands and thousands and thousands  every day of then you can test the  patient  every day and you'll get a negative  result four days in a row until that  patient finally has a viral load that's  clinically significant and then you'll  get a positive test result and you have  caught that patient  in that infection infectious window  so again i think the importance here is  a balance of  clinical sensitivity with clinical  utility  is a more sensitive  assay necessarily better  and then another up question that's up  in the air is does antigen testing  capture the majority of clinically  relevant cases and again i think that  there's no one-size-fits-all answer for  that and it will depend on  the proper evaluation of antigen assays  as they continue to be  released on the market  however i just want to just spend a very  short amount of time  saying that there is good evidence that  anti-antigen testing  correlates with um  viral load so here's a paper from the  international journal of infectious  diseases  and you can see very clearly that sarsko  to antigen response on the y-axis  or antigen level on the y-axis is  linearly and directly correlated with  sars cove  ii viral load um on the on the x-axis  um and also out of a group in the  netherlands published as a pre-print  they look at three  um antigen tests with differing  sensitivities  tests a b and c the vertical dashed  lines  these uh different tests test of these  three uh  so on the x-axis is the limit of  detection basically or the viral load of  the sample  so you can see that test a is more  sensitive than test b  is more sensitive than test c and they  look at  how these tests how good of a job these  tests do  with calling samples that are also  positive by culture and again remember  the idea is  that in order for uh for you to consider  a patient as infectious you have to sort  of  accept that they will be piece culture  positive they may not  uh necessarily be  pcr negative at that point um and so you  can see that  um as the sensitivity decreases actually  you get a bit of a better correlation  to positive cultures than for the more  sensitive tests and this is not  obviously to say that you want to throw  sensitivity out the window  but i think it is important to  at least understand this and balance  sensitivity versus clinical utility  for what it is worth the state of new  york  has recommendations for testing using  antigens  or antigen testing and they are slightly  different depending on whether the  patient is from a symptomatic on the  left or an asymptomatic  patient population on the right  basically in both cases they  tell you that a positive antigen test is  deficient  that patient has covid and they  can they should quarantine for  the appropriate amount of time and then  the difference is how they recommend  dealing with a negative antigen test so  in a symptomatic population  they recommend immediately following a  negative antigen test  with a confirmatory rt-pcr and if that  rtbcr is positive  obviously the patient uh may or may not  be  infectious again think about sensitivity  versus clinical  utility however the recommendation right  now from the state of new york is  to quarantine that patient because of  the positive pcr test  in an asymptomatic patient population  they actually are a little bit less  strong  about their recommendation for pc  following a negative antigen test  with pcr testing they may recommend it  but they're not going to i don't think  enforce it  so this sort of brings me to um  my next slide which is um  using antigen testing in a diagnostic  setting  so i use diagnostic setting to mean  testing of symptomatic patients  and using antigen testing in a screening  setting and i use a screening setting  to mean testing asymptomatic patients so  for example in  um a mass back to school drive  where you want to test a school of a  thousand children  you don't expect them to be positive i  would call this the screening test  and so there have been some studies that  look at the sensitivity of antigen  testing versus pcr  in diagnostic testing uh so in  symptomatic patients  and we see a relatively good up to 90  sensitivity versus pcr in symptomatic  patients which i think is pretty  good um but when we test in asymptomatic  patients for a screening population  we see a much poorer sensitivity some  studies report as low  as 30 versus pcr but again the question  is  does this matter are these clinically  relevant infections i think that's still  something  that moving forward we're going to have  to wrap our heads around  and think about  however i just want to point out one  additional reason of why i think antigen  testing  will be uh important moving forward  so i think pcr testing will remain the  gold standard because  it is a very sensitive methodology  however  if we understand that sometimes more  sensitive is not necessarily better  then i think we have a place for antigen  testing particularly in light  of a recent survey by the association of  molecular pathology amp  who surveyed clinical labs throughout  the country  and inquired about testing  shortages and so you know  many laboratories at least a third of  laboratories  report limited access to either pcr  testing platforms  pcr reagents rna extraction reagents  or consumables for pcr testing  and this has resulted in the majority of  laboratories  running more three or more methods for  covid pcr  which is certainly a burden on the  laboratory  um and also not necessarily ideal for  serial monitoring of patients  who may be tested on different platforms  even within the same laboratory  um one other just quick note is that  these shortages do not just impact  coded testing so because of changes to  supply chains and manufacturing by  manufacturers to ramp up  production of covert related tests  they've had to  oftentimes decrease production of  non-covert related tests  so we've seen significant allocation  issues with reagents for things like std  testing  or other infectious disease testing  viral loads etc  so there are impacts to pc  mass pcr testing that are rippling  through clinical laboratories now  and i do think that if we can transition  some of this testing to antigen testing  hopefully some of these  issues will be mitigated  but to do this we sort of need to make  sure and i just want to end here  by saying what i'm hoping for in terms  of  widespread antigen testing we have to  make sure  that the antigen tests we use are  properly vetted  and they're used appropriately to  replace pcr testing  so for example if you have a highly  symptomatic patient  you probably are will do fine testing  them  by antigen you can save a pcr test  however in order to really know this i  think we need to do a proper  comparison of any antigen test to pcr  and culture to make sure that we have  that right balance between  um sensitivity and clinical relevance  and i do think that at this point the  best  the best location for things like this  to happen are in  clea labs and not at point of care  facilities i think that  just inherently laboratories are better  equipped to  properly evaluate these antigen tests  and i think one important thing moving  forward  will be alternative specimen types and  transport medias  so we have shortages of  vtms and utms we have shortages of  nasopharyngeal swabs  so the ability to test for antigen  in swabs and saline or in saliva  specimens  i think will be key to moving laboratory  testing  to the next stage and where this  pandemic needs to go  and then finally i think that we really  need to have  some high throughput solutions um  and move away from sort of these low  throughput  point of care settings into a high  throughput testing platform  at the clinical laboratory so  thank you for spending this time with me  i would like to thank the wonderful team  of techs that work for me in the  laboratory  particularly led by our lab supervisor  rihanna akhtar  as well as some others in the laboratory  josh ben and ike who have been  instrumental in sort of keeping the  management of the laboratory under  control during this very difficult time  and thanks to uh nelson hayem who  um was our sort of clinical provider who  who helped us with data for our paper  that i talked about on one slide  um and then finally i just would like to  end with a important reminder  please wear your masks  thanks and i'm happy to answer any  questions by email
 

